{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794063147,"nonce":"JG2UVDuuPv4Twgm7Xo4HdlIOXXM=","message":"Follow-up, 2026-11-07, after 43d35c4d deployed. Pixel 10 Pro (WebGL2 worker) via adb + CDP, plus desktop Chrome with the worker forced onto WebGL2.\n\n1. Layout panic gone on the deployed build, but the next failure was 'Mesh Pipeline (OIT Accum)' needing INDEPENDENT_BLEND. Fix in the working tree: the OIT accumulation pipeline is created only when storage buffers exist (WebGL2 has neither); without it BLEND materials draw through the normal premultiplied-alpha pipeline and the OIT clear and composite passes are skipped. Order independence is lost on WebGL2.\n\n2. With that, a local build run on the Pixel renders mesh_3d_demo (helmet) and strangler_demo (fully textured, so the CPU BC expansion works on the device) over adb reverse. buster_drone loads in about a minute.\n\n3. Missing drone shadow on WebGL2 (reported on a phone; reproduced on desktop forced-WebGL2). Cause: mesh_dt.wgsl declared the old single-light Uniforms layout while the Rust side uploads the current one (four lights, light_count, morph fields). On WebGL2 shadow_enabled, has_texture, has_normal_map, displacement_scale, normal_scale and has_skinning were read from the wrong offsets; shadow_enabled landed in an unused light slot and read 0 for every mesh. Confirmed with a debug build that output shadow_factor, in-range and shadow_enabled as colour. Fix in the working tree: Uniforms now matches mesh.wgsl, key light read from lights[0]. The drop shadow and the body decals are back on forced-WebGL2. Not yet re-checked on the phone. The DT shader still lights with the key light only; fill and rim lights are not applied on WebGL2.\n\n4. Frame pacing of the skeleton demos on the Pixel (WebGL2): worker rAF rate 82 fps for the helmet (1 mesh), 41 for the strangler, 27 for the drone (39 meshes); the drone's render closure sees dt 26-83 ms (median 40), and the clip clock advances by exactly dt each frame, so animation time is correct and the cost is per-frame render time. Desktop WebGPU is a steady 16.7 ms. Frame rate scales with mesh count, consistent with per-mesh render passes (each mesh begins its own pass over the full-size HDR target and submits separately) plus per-mesh joint-texture uploads; not proven. There is no fps cap on the web runner: animation_fps_cap in the demos is read only by native windowed.rs, and the game kit has no frame-rate constant. A rotor authored at ~30 keyframes/s sampled at 25-40 fps strobes.","files":null}]}